## Core Language Concepts <a name="core-concepts"></a>

### 1. Namespaces & Structure

**Purpose**: Organize code and avoid naming conflicts

```csharp
// Namespace declaration
namespace CompanyName.ProjectName.Feature
{
    // Code goes here
}

// Using statements (imports)
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

// Aliasing
using List = System.Collections.Generic.List<int>;

// File-scoped namespace (C# 11+)
namespace CompanyName.ProjectName;

// Then classes don't need extra braces
public class MyClass { }
```

**Benefits**:
- Organize code logically
- Prevent naming collisions
- Improve maintainability
- Support large projects

### 2. Classes & Objects

**Class Definition**:
```csharp
public class Employee
{
    // Fields (data)
    private string _name;
    private int _employeeId;
    private decimal _salary;
    
    // Properties (with getter/setter)
    public string Name
    {
        get { return _name; }
        set { _name = value; }
    }
    
    // Auto-property (C# 3.0+)
    public int EmployeeId { get; set; }
    
    // Init-only property (C# 9.0+)
    public string Department { get; init; }
    
    // Constructor
    public Employee(string name, int id)
    {
        _name = name;
        _employeeId = id;
    }
    
    // Method
    public void DisplayInfo()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Name: {Name}, ID: {EmployeeId}");
    }
    
    // Property with backing field
    public decimal Salary
    {
        get { return _salary; }
        set 
        { 
            if (value > 0)
                _salary = value;
        }
    }
}

// Usage
var employee = new Employee("John", 101)
{
    EmployeeId = 101,
    Department = "Sales"
};
employee.DisplayInfo();
```

**Theory**:
- **Fields**: Hold data, typically private
- **Properties**: Controlled access to fields
- **Methods**: Define behavior
- **Constructors**: Initialize objects

