## Object-Oriented Programming <a name="oop"></a>

### 1. Inheritance

**Concept**: Child class inherits from parent class

```csharp
// Base class
public class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    
    public virtual void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some sound");
    }
}

// Derived class
public class Dog : Animal
{
    // Override parent method
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
    
    // New method
    public void Fetch()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Fetching ball...");
    }
}

// Usage
Animal animal = new Dog { Name = "Rex" };
animal.MakeSound(); // Output: Woof!

Dog dog = animal as Dog;
if (dog != null)
    dog.Fetch(); // Output: Fetching ball...
```

**Key Concepts**:
- **Base class**: Parent with common functionality
- **Derived class**: Child inheriting from base
- **Virtual method**: Can be overridden
- **Override keyword**: Provide new implementation
- **is/as operators**: Type checking and casting

### 2. Polymorphism

**Concept**: Objects can take many forms

```csharp
// Shape hierarchy
public abstract class Shape
{
    public abstract double GetArea();
    public abstract void Draw();
}

public class Circle : Shape
{
    public double Radius { get; set; }
    
    public override double GetArea()
    {
        return Math.PI * Radius * Radius;
    }
    
    public override void Draw()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Drawing circle");
    }
}

public class Rectangle : Shape
{
    public double Width { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }
    
    public override double GetArea()
    {
        return Width * Height;
    }
    
    public override void Draw()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Drawing rectangle");
    }
}

// Polymorphic usage
List<Shape> shapes = new List<Shape>
{
    new Circle { Radius = 5 },
    new Rectangle { Width = 10, Height = 20 }
};

foreach (var shape in shapes)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Area: {shape.GetArea()}");
    shape.Draw();
}
```

**Benefits**:
- Write generic code
- Easy to extend
- Flexible designs
- Reduced coupling

### 3. Encapsulation

**Concept**: Hide internal details, expose interfaces

```csharp
public class BankAccount
{
    // Private field - hidden from outside
    private decimal _balance;
    
    // Public property - controlled access
    public decimal Balance
    {
        get { return _balance; }
        private set { _balance = value; }
    }
    
    // Controlled method
    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        _balance += amount;
    }
    
    public void Withdraw(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount > _balance)
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Insufficient funds");
        _balance -= amount;
    }
}

// Usage
var account = new BankAccount();
account.Deposit(1000); // OK
account.Withdraw(500); // OK
// account.Balance = -100; // ERROR: Can't set directly
```

**Access Modifiers**:
- `public`: Accessible everywhere
- `private`: Only in this class
- `protected`: In this class and derived classes
- `internal`: In same assembly
- `protected internal`: In same assembly or derived classes

### 4. Abstraction

**Concept**: Define what, not how

```csharp
// Abstract class - defines contract
public abstract class PaymentProcessor
{
    public abstract void ProcessPayment(decimal amount);
    public abstract bool ValidateCard(string cardNumber);
    
    // Concrete method (optional)
    public void LogTransaction(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Processing payment of ${amount}");
    }
}

// Implementation
public class CreditCardProcessor : PaymentProcessor
{
    public override void ProcessPayment(decimal amount)
    {
        ValidateCard("1234-5678-9012-3456");
        Console.WriteLine($"Processing credit card payment: ${amount}");
    }
    
    public override bool ValidateCard(string cardNumber)
    {
        return cardNumber.Length == 19; // Simplified
    }
}

// Interface - pure contract
public interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message);
    void LogError(string error);
}

public class FileLogger : ILogger
{
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        System.IO.File.AppendAllText("log.txt", message);
    }
    
    public void LogError(string error)
    {
        System.IO.File.AppendAllText("error.txt", error);
    }
}
```

